  "Question": "In rectangle \\(ABCD\\), \\(AB=6\\) and \\(BC=8\\). The diagonals intersect at \\(O\\). Find the area of \\(\\triangle OAB\\).",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "12",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The diagonals divide the rectangle into four triangles of equal area. The rectangle has area \\(48\\), so the area of \\(\\triangle OAB\\) is \\(48\\div4=12\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Find the triangle's height\n\nThe diagonals bisect each other, so \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(AC\\). Let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(AB\\). In \\(\\triangle ABC\\), the segment \\(OM\\) joins two side midpoints, so \\(OM\\parallel BC\\) and \\(OM=BC/2=4\\). Since \\(BC\\perp AB\\), \\(OM\\) is the height of \\(\\triangle OAB\\) above base \\(AB\\). Its area is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac12 AB\\cdot OM=\\frac12\\cdot6\\cdot4=12.\n\\]",
